# q07 — DELIBERATE ERROR. Two files claim the same type and the same
# interface.
#
# `store/vault.lume` line 10 writes `extend {T} with Bagged[T]:`.
# `store/annex.lume` line 10 writes `extend {T} with Bagged[T]:` again,
# with a different body. This entry imports both, so both are in one
# program.
#
# Meant to be REFUSED.
#
# EXPECTED ERROR: a duplicate-`extend` error, reported at one of the two
# `extend` lines — I would report the second one the loader reaches,
# `corpus/m30/q07_clash/store/annex.lume:10` — and NAMING BOTH FILES, the
# other being `corpus/m30/q07_clash/store/vault.lume:10`. The README
# promises exactly this: "two `extend`s claiming the same type and the
# same interface anywhere in one program is an error naming both files."
#
# What I want to see in the message: both paths, both line numbers, the
# type (`{T}`) and the interface (`Bagged`) spelled out, and no mention of
# `main.lume`, which is innocent — it only imports.
#
# What would be a bad message: an error at the `import store.annex` line
# of this file; an error naming only one file; a "duplicate method `held` on
# `{T}`" that never says the word `extend`; or, worst, no error at all
# with one of the two bodies silently winning (then `puts nums.held_list`
# below would print either "3, 1, 2" or "2, 1, 3" depending on which).

import store.vault
import store.annex

def main:
  nums = {3, 1, 2}
  puts vault.summary({"a", "b"})
  puts annex.loudly({"a", "b"})
  puts nums.held_count
  puts nums.held_list
